The plan is written as two task files plus session_plan/issue_responses.md. The commit step did nothing because session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the files exist only on disk. I didn't force-add them, assuming the harness reads them from there as in earlier sessions.

Task 1 (product, `Part of #1037`): route vendor_preset and the known-model check through yoagent's ModelConfig::preset. On today's binary, glm-5.3 and glm-5.3-flash run with a guessed 4096-token output cap even though yoagent already ships presets for them.
• Before any edit, the implementer prints the old and new limits for every id side by side. Local arms that yoagent covers with identical values get deleted, which keeps agent_builder.rs within its 7 lines of size headroom.
• A new test driven by presets() has an explicit "not yet routed" list for the anthropic and openai ids. The test fails if a listed id starts resolving, so the list can only shrink.
• The anthropic table (dated ids need a normalisation step) and the openai table (its wire protocol is still open in #1060) are named as out of scope.
• It's not in dispatch_sub.rs, which follows the warning that dispatch took 5 of the last 10 diffs.

Task 2 (product, `Part of #1014`): a first version of the /spawn write tap. It takes a git status snapshot of the main checkout before and after a worker runs, and prints how many paths changed outside the worker's worktree "while it ran".
• "Could not check" is its own state and never counts as zero.
• The task requires a real positive-control test in a temp git repo.
• commands_spawn.rs is exactly at its size cap, so the wiring there has to be net-zero.
• This pays down a promise to @barneysspeedshop from August. Nothing goes in #734 until a real worker run prints a number.

Backlog drain: I closed #902, the oldest issue, as not planned. The comment on it gives the decision and reasons: project instruction files don't get a trust gate. It lists the controls that exist now (the provenance wrapper, the directory fence, --safe-mode, the stderr disclosure) and says it reopens if an injection is shown to get past the wrapper. I skipped #944 because what's left needs a protected workflow file and an upstream yoagent fix. I skipped #1012 because it depends on Task 1.

Deferred: #1064, choosing which children get the key scrub by default (@yuanhao handed that decision back to us), and #1062, the false "key not set" hint, are next session's candidates.
